# Windows PowerShell scripts: CRLF line endings. The files are committed with a
# UTF-8 BOM as part of their content — Windows PowerShell 5.1 otherwise decodes
# BOM-less files as Windows-1252 and corrupts non-ASCII characters inside string
# literals (which breaks parsing). Do not strip the BOM when editing.
#
# EXCEPTION — windows/install.ps1 and windows/uninstall.ps1 must stay BOM-LESS
# and ASCII-only: they are fetched via `irm <url> | iex` (the README one-liners),
# and irm keeps a BOM as a stray U+FEFF that makes iex fail on the first token
# (fixed in 762dcc0, regressed once by re-applying this rule mechanically).
# ASCII-only keeps them safe to run from a local clone without a BOM.
*.ps1 text eol=crlf

# Unix shell scripts must keep LF, or shebangs break on Linux/macOS.
*.sh  text eol=lf

*.md   text
*.json text

*.png binary
